4a7d6e35-c4ad-4592-b482-b2ef8f18de0e.Milestone/Target Description:
Measures to counter corruption and increase integrity shall include:

1. In relation to state-owned enterprises:
1.1. Entry into force of legal act(s) to introduce the obligation of putting in place corruption risk management systems in state-owned enterprises. The corruption risk management systems shall cover the selection of senior officials, measures to prevent conflict of interest, methodology to assess and control corruption risks, ethics and integrity trainings of officials, and introducing the role of an integrity officer;
1.2. Adoption of a Code of Ethical Conduct.

2. With regard to civil servants holding positions of high corruption risk in the central executive branch:
2.1. Identification of positions of high corruption risk for which integrity checks should be carried out;
2.2. Entry into force of legal act(s) stating the obligation to carry out integrity tests for civil servants at least in the following circumstances:
•	upon appointment;
•	on promotion to a higher management position;
•	based on the results of an inspection carried out by the inspectorates.
2.3. Preparation of an analysis of the definition of conflicts of interest under national legislation and recommendation(s) for possible improvement(s).

4a7d6e35-c4ad-4592-b482-b2ef8f18de0e.Qualitative Indicator:
Provisions in the legal act(s) indicating the entry into force;
Adopted Code of Ethical Conduct;
Analysis of legislation on conflicts of interest

4a7d6e35-c4ad-4592-b482-b2ef8f18de0e.Milestone/Target Description:
Entry into force of legal acts, which shall provide for:
- early warning tools;
- facilitating the opening and conducting of insolvency and restructuring procedures;
- duties of the directors in case of likelihood of insolvency;
- a possibility for electronic exchange of information and documents in the insolvency, restructuring and discharge of debt procedures;
- stricter regulation of the profession of insolvency practitioners to ensure they have the necessary expertise and that the conditions for eligibility, as well as the process for the appointment, removal and resignation of 
practitioners are clear, transparent and fair.

4a7d6e35-c4ad-4592-b482-b2ef8f18de0e.Qualitative Indicator:
1. Trainings organised; 
2. Electronic tool(s) upgraded;
3. Entry into force of legal act(s) and adoption of guidelines; 
4. Publication of statistical data

1. Adoption or revision  of  supervisory strategic documents;
2. Adoption or revision of amendments to the supervisory procedures or manuals; 
3. Adoption of guidance on the approach to politically exposed persons;
4. Provisions in the law indicating the entry into force of the amendments

4a7d6e35-c4ad-4592-b482-b2ef8f18de0e.Milestone/Target Description:
The changes to the Public Procurement Agency’s methodology shall:
•	increase the weighting of the risk factor for procurement procedures supported by EU funds;
•	introduce new risk factors for selecting procedures for checks;
•	introduce template(s) for reporting and a procedure for the publication of the outcome of the checks.

The amendments to the legal act(s) shall provide for:
-	revisions to the eligibility and work requirement criteria related to the minimum income scheme, including reducing the required registration time at the employment services from 6 to 3 months, and introducing incentives to uptake employment ;
-	linking the Differentiated Minimum Income (DMI) to the annually updated at-risk-of-poverty threshold;
-	the at-risk-of-poverty threshold shall be harmonized with the EUROSTAT methodology;
-	ensurance that for each target group of the scheme, the DMI is computed by multiplying a group-specific coefficient by an anchoring element, common to all target groups: 
o	for each target group, the group-specific coefficient shall not be lower than its 2021 value;
o	the anchoring element shall be equal to at least 30% of the most recent AROP threshold;
-	introduction of the legal definition of ‘economically inactive’;
-	provisions specifying the activities for the activation of the ‘economically inactive’.

The National Map of Social Services shall cover:
-	a list of social services at the municipal and district level funded fully or partly from the state budget;
-	the maximum number of users for each social service for which funding is provided fully or partly from the state budget.

The Employment Agency shall have access to:
-	the National Database information system;
-	the digital laboratory platform; 
-	the application, which lists job trainings and vacancies; 
-	software for processing survey results; 
-	the digital services concerning employment subsidies, trainings and registration procedures for job seekers.
The digital laboratory and the application, which lists job trainings and vacancies shall be publicly available.
In addition, 428 tablets and ID card readers shall be delivered.

The legal act(s) shall provide for: 
-	a revision of the organisation and management structure of the National Culture Fund;
-	monitoring of the activities of the National Culture Fund.

The National Health Information System (NHIS) shall be upgraded by adding new functionalities related to services concerning: 
- electronic medical records of citizens;
- e-prescriptions and e-referrals modules;
- a system for the collection of data from hospitals.

The legal act(s) shall:
- introduce a methodology for determining the number of nursing and/or midwifery professionals, required in 
different types of healthcare facilities;
- abolish university fees for students in nursing and midwifery programmes; 
- provide that the personnel costs for health professionals in providers of hospital and outpatient medical care which have contracts with the National Health Insurance Fund are determined according to their qualifications;
- offer free training of health professionals who commit to work in outpatient clinics in regions with limited 
access to healthcare; 
- introduce the possibility for nursing and midwifery professionals, rehabilitators and doctor’s assistants to establish their own practice; 
- provide for the invitation for participation of the professional organisation of nurses, midwives and associated health professionals in the process of negotiations with the National Health Insurance Fund (NHIF).
- introduce health care services performed for newborns up to 6 months after discharge from the hospital in the package of outpatient care activities financed by the NHIF provided by nursing and midwifery 
professionals via their own private practice.

- Construction and/or renovation works for six Group 2 centres for interventional diagnosis and endovascular treatment of cerebrovascular diseases shall be carried out;
- equipment in the six Group 2 centres shall be supplied;
- equipment for 17 Group 1 medical centres shall be supplied. 

The categorisation of Group 1 and Group 2 medical centres shall follow the definition in the Concept for the 
Development of Centres for Interventional Diagnostics and Endovascular Treatment of Cerebrovascular Diseases.

The contract(s) shall cover the development of the National Digital Platform for Medical Diagnostics with the following:
- the uploading of medical images; 
- the processing of the medical images by a machine learning algorithm.

Legal act(s) shall 
- designate a responsible body for maintaining an information system of energy poor households and energy vulnerable customers; 
- establish a responsible body for designing measures tackling energy poverty in view of a retail electricity market liberalisation. The legal act(s) shall specify that the responsible body shall design a programme in relation to reducing energy poverty within the first six months of its establishment.

In relation to the National Palace of Culture and the Festival and Congress Centre – Varna, the following shall be delivered:
- 66 LED displays and a software for their control;
- Platform for digitalisation of business processes;
- Network equipment and cyber-security platform.

The Ministry of Finance, in cooperation with the relevant stakeholders, shall draw up a Strategic Framework for the Development of the Capital Market in the Republic of Croatia and   an Action Plan, to be adopted by the Croatian Government.
The Action Plan shall accompany the Strategic Framework and shall include timelines for the implementation of individual activities. The Strategy (the Strategic Framework for the Development of the Capital Market in Croatia) shall provide an analysis of the state of the capital market in Croatia, identifying key obstacles to its functioning as an alternative source of financing and opportunities for its diversification and development. At least the following elements shall be addressed: 
i.	The role of large institutional investors in the capital market, including second pillar institutions (in particular, mandatory pension funds and pension insurance companies) and insurance companies; 
ii.	Expanding the possibilities and simplifying the procedures for listing, taking into account the state-owned enterprises, SMEs and mid-caps; 
iii.	Increasing liquidity, diversification and turnover in the capital market, including fostering market making activity; 
iv.	Fostering a deeper market for financial instruments, such as corporate bonds and government bonds, as well as commodity derivatives and securitisations; 
v.	Developing existing financial market infrastructures, including options to improve the business model of the central counterparty; 
vi.	Adapting the regulatory framework to better respond to the objective of developing the capital market; 
vii.	Strengthening the investors’ financial literacy; 
viii.	Strengthening the framework for sustainable finance.

4a7d6e35-c4ad-4592-b482-b2ef8f18de0e.Qualitative Indicator:
Entry into force of four legal amendments to: i) Service Areas Regulation; 
ii) Regulation on Evaluation of Performance of Water Operators; iii) Regulation on 
the methodology for determining the pricing of water services; iv) Regulation on specific conditions for the provision of water services, which will reform public water operators;

4a7d6e35-c4ad-4592-b482-b2ef8f18de0e.Milestone/Target Description:
The Amendments to the Roads Act, shall inter alia:
-Ensure the interoperability of electronic road toll systems on the whole Union road network, urban and interurban motorways, major and secondary roads and different structures, such as tunnels or bridges, and ferries;
-Facilitate the cross-border exchange of vehicle registration data concerning vehicles and owners or holders of vehicles for which failure to pay any type of road toll has been identified in the Union;
-Include provisions fostering increase of infrastructure used for bicycle traffic and pedestrian traffic;
-Set criteria and conditions on pay toll cost coverage;
-Address the exemptions from the obligation to pay the fees for the right to build and the right of easement on a public road, by removing these exemptions from the legislation. This shall create a level playing field in relation to such fees for all entrepreneurs.

4a7d6e35-c4ad-4592-b482-b2ef8f18de0e.Measure Description:
The reform aims at increasing the competitiveness and efficiency of the rail sector in order to provide better services to passenger and freight customers and increase Croatia’s economic competitiveness. To underpin the reform, the Croatian Government shall adopt a Sectoral Policy Letter for the Railway Sector by 30 June 2021, presenting the way forward in key areas related to the reform and modernisation of the railway sector, and establishing implementation plans for the government and railway companies, including detailed measures and actions as regards:
The governance of the sector;
The management of railway companies and operations;
Sectoral investment and financing planning;
Development of knowledge, technologies and skills in the railway sector.
The reform shall be completed by 31 December 2025.

